WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.970
Okay gang...let's take a spoiler-filled dive
into a scene in Avengers: Endgame that had

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.379
audiences cheering.

00:00:06.379 --> 00:00:09.600
There were a lot of those, as well as scenes
that made people cry.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.890
Some scenes made you do both.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:14.660
But one specific moment was something fans
of the MCU have been waiting to happen for

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.510
at least four years: Captain America lifting
Thor's hammer.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:22.900
When Thor goes back to 2013 to recover the
Aether, he takes the opportunity to borrow

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:28.499
his trusty hammer Mjolnir, which was destroyed
by his sister Hela early on in 2017's Thor:

00:00:28.499 --> 00:00:29.499
Ragnarok.

00:00:29.499 --> 00:00:33.630
He arrives at the final battle with both the
hammer and his new axe Stormbreaker.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:37.340
Even though he's let himself go a little bit
in the last five years, one would think that

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:42.520
a God of Thunder dual-wielding his magic weapons
would be too much for Thanos to handle.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:47.480
Not so, it turns out even without the power-boosting
qualities of any of the Infinity Stones, the

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.850
Mad Titan easily gained the upper hand, stripping
Thor of both his weapons.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:55.890
But just when it appeared that our favorite
Asgardian was getting ready to be unceremoniously

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:00.571
dispatched to Valhalla, the scene cut to Mjolnir
being recovered by someone and the reveal

00:01:00.571 --> 00:01:05.239
of that someone being none other than Captain
America had moviegoers on their feet.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:09.220
You may remember that Cap had been the only
Avenger capable of even budging the hammer

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:13.480
during the lively party near the opening of
2015's Avengers: Age of Ultron.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:18.110
All of a sudden, here was Steve Rogers as
Captain Thor, calling down lightning strikes

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:22.890
and using Mjolnir in conjunction with his
shield to give Thanos a serious headache.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:27.980
As we all know, whosoever holds Mjolnir, if
he be worthy, shall possess the power of Thor

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:32.280
— and Rogers' ability to wield the mighty
weapon at a most opportune moment raises a

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.659
couple of questions.

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:38.780
In the comics, the first time Cap was seen
to be worthy of Mjolnir was way back in 1988,

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.000
in Thor #390.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.060
It was during Rogers' stint as "The Captain"
after he'd had his more famous codename stripped

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.320
from him by the U.S.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.320
Government.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.350
During a visit by Thor to the Avengers' base,
he's ambushed by the evil god Seth and his

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:55.240
minions, becoming separated from his hammer
and he's shocked when "The Captain" is able

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:00.510
to briefly wield it, smashing a few enemies
before returning Mjolnir to its rightful owner.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:04.170
After prevailing in the fight, Thor was revealed
to be totally psyched that his friend had

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:08.840
proven himself worthy, telling Cap, "A sacred
bond unites all those who have ever been privileged

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:12.480
to wield Mjolnir, a bond which stretches far
into infinity."

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.069
Cap was also seen making use of the weapon
on several other occasions, including a What

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:18.069
If?

00:02:18.069 --> 00:02:22.470
alternate universe scenario involving the
Age of Apocalypse storyline in 2006, and later

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:27.060
in the Fear Itself arc. which featured a splash
page of Cap calling down lightning much like

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.450
he does in Endgame.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:32.650
Even when his body was purged of the Super
Soldier Serum in the Secret Empire series,

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:36.810
he was still able to hoist Mjolnir suggesting
that even when physically unable to throw

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.870
down properly, Rogers still has a warrior's
heart.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:42.780
So: what does this mean for the MCU version
of Cap?

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.880
The moment in which he was able to lift the
weapon ever-so-slightly in Age of Ultron was

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.880
played mostly for laughs, as evidenced by
the look on Thor's face, which is not so much

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.940
"I knew it!" as it is "Oh, crap".

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.570
But, think about it: no other Avenger, despite
all of them being heroes, could make Mjolnir

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:03.100
move even a millimeter suggesting that it
takes much more than merely being a hero in

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.709
order to be deemed worthy.

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:09.020
Consider the Vision, who, despite being an
android, was probably the most benevolent,

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.370
pure-hearted Avenger.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:14.080
He was shown to be able to pick up Mjolnir
and toss it around as if it were nothing.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.290
This is the same Vision who explicitly insisted
on sacrificing himself for the good of the

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:21.470
universe during the events of Avengers: Infinity
War.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:25.660
His example may bring us closer to the real
reason why Rogers is worthy of wielding the

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:26.790
hammer too.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:31.680
Remember that way back in 2011's Thor, Odinson
himself was unable to hoist Mjolnir for a

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.709
good portion of the film, as he had become
lost in his single-minded quest to become

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:38.790
King of Asgard, resulting in Odin banishing
him to Earth.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:42.830
It was only when Thor set aside his ego and
displayed a willingness to face death for

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:46.410
the good of others that he was again able
to call the weapon to him.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:51.300
Now, remember the incident in that same year's
Captain America: The First Avenger which cemented

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:55.560
Dr. Abraham Erskine's decision to infuse Rogers
with the Super Soldier Serum.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.519
Tommy Lee Jones' Colonel Phillips tossed a
dummy grenade into the middle of a crowded

00:03:59.519 --> 00:04:04.020
training exercise — and watched Rogers,
the smallest and weakest of the bunch by a

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.600
mile, be the only soldier not to scatter.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.989
Instead, he fell on the grenade, displaying
an unhesitating willingness to give his life

00:04:10.989 --> 00:04:14.430
for his fellow soldiers before even setting
foot on a battlefield.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:22.710
"Not a perfect soldier, but a good man."

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:27.250
Perhaps it's this bottomless capacity for
self-sacrifice which makes one truly worthy

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.250
of Mjolnir.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:32.350
If that's the case, then why was he unable
to move it any more than a quarter-inch during

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.000
the party in Age of Ultron?

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.630
In our minds, there's only one possible answer:
modesty.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.160
What if Steve could have hoisted the hammer
at that point?

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.080
What if he deliberately pretended not to be
able to lift it in order to avoid making his

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.560
fellow Avengers look bad especially the prideful
Thor?

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:48.560
Seem far-fetched?

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.590
Well, we are talking about the moral center
of the entire Marvel Universe here.

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:56.770
That's why we'll always love Steve Rogers
he's not in this superhero life for the glory

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:57.770
of it.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:59.520
He's in it for everyone else.

